## AUTHENTICATION & SECURITY

### Q: How does the login work?

**A:** The login page collects email/username, password, and a role selector (Author/Editor/Admin). When the user clicks Sign In:
1. The frontend sends a `POST /api/method/login` request to the Frappe backend with the credentials.
2. Frappe validates the credentials against its User doctype in MariaDB.
3. On success, Frappe returns a **session cookie** (`sid`) and a **CSRF token** cookie (`csrf_token`).
4. My frontend captures the CSRF token from `document.cookie` and stores it in memory.
5. Then it calls [getUserInfo()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/erpnextClient.ts#167-200) — which first calls `frappe.auth.get_logged_user` to get the email, then fetches the full User document to get the `full_name` and `roles` array.
6. The roles are mapped: if the user has "Manuscript Admin" or "System Manager" → admin; "Manuscript Editor" → editor; otherwise → author.
7. The user is redirected to their role-specific home page.

### Q: What authentication method did you use and why?

**A:** I use **cookie-based session authentication** provided by Frappe. Every API call includes `credentials: "include"` so the browser automatically sends the session cookie. This is more secure than storing tokens in localStorage because cookies can be marked HttpOnly (not accessible via JavaScript) and are automatically managed by the browser. I also include a **CSRF token** in every request header (`X-Frappe-CSRF-Token`) to prevent Cross-Site Request Forgery attacks.

### Q: How does session persistence work?

**A:** When the app loads, the [AppProvider](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/store/app-store.tsx#29-122) runs a [checkSession()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/store/app-store.tsx#49-65) function in a `useEffect` on mount. It calls [getUserInfo()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/erpnextClient.ts#167-200) which tries to hit the Frappe backend using the existing cookie. If the cookie is still valid, Frappe returns the user info, and I re-hydrate the user state — so the user stays logged in even after refreshing the page. If the cookie has expired, the call fails silently and the user sees the login page.

### Q: How does logout work?

**A:** Logout sends a `POST /api/method/logout` to the Frappe backend, which destroys the server-side session. On the client side, I clear the CSRF token from memory, set the user state to `null`, clear the manuscript list, and navigate back to the login page. The user can log out from either the Settings page ("Sign out" button) or the dropdown menu in the header.

### Q: How do you handle CSRF protection?

**A:** After login, Frappe sets a `csrf_token` cookie. My [erpnextClient.ts](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/erpnextClient.ts) reads this cookie using `document.cookie.match(/csrf_token=([^;]+)/)` and attaches it as the `X-Frappe-CSRF-Token` header on every API request. This ensures that only requests originating from my frontend (which has access to the cookie) can modify data.

