## FRONTEND-BACKEND COMMUNICATION

### Q: How does the frontend communicate with the backend?

**A:** The frontend has a two-layer API architecture:
1. **[erpnextClient.ts](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/erpnextClient.ts)** — Low-level client that wraps [fetch()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/server.ts#48-61). It handles URL construction, CSRF tokens, cookie credentials, JSON parsing, and error handling. It exposes [login()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/erpnextClient.ts#114-145), [logout()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/erpnextClient.ts#146-153), [getLoggedUser()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/erpnextClient.ts#154-166), [getUserInfo()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/erpnextClient.ts#167-200), and a generic [call()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/erpnextClient.ts#98-113) function.
2. **[manuscriptService.ts](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/manuscriptService.ts)** — Business-level service that calls `erpnextClient.call()` with the specific endpoint names and parameters. It exposes [listManuscripts()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/manuscriptService.ts#57-71), [createManuscript()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/manuscriptService.ts#126-153), [updateState()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/manuscriptService.ts#154-167), [assignEditor()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/manuscriptService.ts#168-179), [addEditorDecision()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/manuscriptService.ts#180-195), [uploadRevision()](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/manuscriptService.ts#196-207), etc.

### Q: How do you handle CORS?

**A:** In development, I use **Vite's built-in proxy**. The [vite.config.ts](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/vite.config.ts) proxies all `/api/*` requests to `http://localhost:8080` (where ERPNext Docker is running). This means the browser thinks it's calling the same origin, so there are no CORS issues. In production, you either set `VITE_ERPNEXT_URL` to the ERPNext domain or use a reverse proxy (like Nginx).

### Q: How do you handle API errors?

**A:** I have a custom [FrappeError](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/erpnextClient.ts#24-37) class that extends JavaScript's [Error](file:///c:/Users/rahul/OneDrive/Booksubmission_grp2/manuscript-buddy/src/services/erpnextClient.ts#24-37). When the backend returns a non-200 response, I parse the response body — Frappe can return errors in `_server_messages` (a JSON array of message objects) or in `exception`. I extract the human-readable message, the HTTP status code, and the exception type. On the frontend, each page catches these errors and shows them using toast notifications (Sonner) or inline error messages.

